From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: tag_vsc73xx_8021q: use the bridge VID for unicast
 transmitted directly to a bridged port

Joining a bridge replaces a port's standalone tag_8021q VLAN with the
bridge's VLAN (dsa_tag_8021q_bridge_join() adds the bridge VID and
deletes the standalone one). The xmit path, however, only uses
the bridge VID when skb->offload_fwd_mark is set, and otherwise tags with
the standalone VID.

That is not reachable for unicast while everything goes through the
bridge, because bridge-forwarded frames carry the mark (IGMP/MLD are the
exception, see below). It is reachable as soon as something transmits
unicast straight onto the user port instead: the netfilter flowtable's
direct transmit path does exactly this once dev_fill_forward_path() has
resolved a bridge port. Two things then go wrong:

 - unless the driver keeps the standalone VLAN, the switch no longer has
   that VID for the port and drops the frame;
 - even where it is kept, the port is usually the wrong one. Bridged
   traffic is received on the bridge VID, which does not identify the
   ingress port, so dsa_tag_8021q_find_user() attributes it to the first
   forwarding port of the bridge and the software FDB learns every
   station behind the switch there.

Measured on an IPQ5332 board with an RTL8372N switch (rtl837x, which
reuses this tagger): with the flowtable forwarding through the bridge,
frames towards the client carry the bridge VID 3088 and arrive; with the
flowtable transmitting directly to the port, they carry the standalone
VID 3076 and the client receives nothing, while the switch's own FDB has
the client on a different port entirely.

So for an unmarked unicast frame to a port of a VLAN-unaware bridge, use
the bridge VID as well and let the switch select the egress port from
its own FDB, exactly as it does for bridge-forwarded traffic. This also
applies to unicast deliberately sent on one port, e.g. through a packet
socket: it goes where the switch's FDB has the destination, or is
flooded within the bridge if the destination is unknown, rather than out
of the port it was sent on.

Multicast and broadcast keep the standalone VID. Group-addressed
link-local traffic (STP, LLDP, EAPOL, PTP) is meant for one specific
link, and the bridge sends some frames unmarked once per port, such as
IGMP/MLD (nbp_switchdev_can_offload_tx_fwd() refuses to offload them):
with the bridge VID the switch would flood every copy to the whole
bridge. These frames leave a bridged port only if the driver keeps the
standalone VLAN in hardware while the port is bridged; otherwise they
are dropped, as before this patch.

Everything else is left as it was. Marked frames keep their existing
handling in full: the bridge transmits only one copy per offloaded
hardware domain and relies on the switch to flood it, so they must not be
narrowed to a single port. VLAN-aware bridges are untouched as well --
there every forwarded frame carries one of the bridge's own VLANs and
tag_8021q does not decide the egress port.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
[François Guerraz: restrict to unicast so the per-port multicast copies
 the bridge sends unmarked are not flooded; describe the receive
 attribution that makes the stack's port choice unreliable]
Signed-off-by: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
---
--- a/net/dsa/tag_vsc73xx_8021q.c
+++ b/net/dsa/tag_vsc73xx_8021q.c
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 /* Copyright (C) 2024 Pawel Dembicki <paweldembicki@gmail.com>
  */
 #include <linux/dsa/8021q.h>
+#include <linux/etherdevice.h>
 
 #include "tag.h"
 #include "tag_8021q.h"
@@ -13,17 +14,41 @@
 {
 	struct dsa_port *dp = dsa_user_to_port(netdev);
 	u16 queue_mapping = skb_get_queue_mapping(skb);
+	unsigned int bridge_num = dsa_port_bridge_num_get(dp);
+	struct net_device *br = dsa_port_bridge_dev_get(dp);
 	u16 tx_vid = dsa_tag_8021q_standalone_vid(dp);
 	u8 pcp;
 
+	/* A frame the bridge forwarded or flooded is marked, and for an
+	 * offloaded hardware domain the bridge transmits only one copy and
+	 * relies on the switch to flood it. Leave that path exactly as it
+	 * was.
+	 */
 	if (skb->offload_fwd_mark) {
-		unsigned int bridge_num = dsa_port_bridge_num_get(dp);
-		struct net_device *br = dsa_port_bridge_dev_get(dp);
-
 		if (br_vlan_enabled(br))
 			return skb;
 
 		tx_vid = dsa_tag_8021q_bridge_vid(bridge_num);
+	} else if (bridge_num && br && !br_vlan_enabled(br) &&
+		   is_unicast_ether_addr(eth_hdr(skb)->h_dest)) {
+		/* An unmarked unicast frame bypassed bridge forwarding and was
+		 * transmitted straight onto a port of a VLAN-unaware bridge --
+		 * the netfilter flowtable's direct transmit path does exactly
+		 * this. The port it was given is not reliable: bridged traffic
+		 * is received on the bridge VID, which does not identify the
+		 * ingress port, so the software FDB learns every station behind
+		 * the switch on one port. Use the bridge VID and let the switch
+		 * pick the egress port from its own FDB, as it does for
+		 * bridge-forwarded frames.
+		 *
+		 * Multicast and broadcast keep the standalone VID. Link-local
+		 * group frames (STP, LLDP, EAPOL) are for one specific link,
+		 * and the bridge sends some frames unmarked once per port
+		 * (IGMP/MLD), so the bridge VID would make the switch flood
+		 * each copy to the whole bridge. They only leave the port if
+		 * the driver keeps the standalone VLAN while it is bridged.
+		 */
+		tx_vid = dsa_tag_8021q_bridge_vid(bridge_num);
 	}
 
 	pcp = netdev_txq_to_tc(netdev, queue_mapping);
